1 OCCU Lensgreve till Gyldensteen. Danmarks udenrigsminister i to perioderne 1773-80 og 1784-97. 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Andreas Peter von Bernstorff''', lensgreve til Gyldensteen, Statsminister i Danmark
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 3 Dec 1762 Copenhagen, Denmark to [http://www.geni.com/people/Henriette-Katharina-zu-Stolberg-Stolberg/6000000001504528096 Henriette Frederikke reichgraffin Stolberg zu Stolberg] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children===
2 CONT
2 CONT # Andreas Ditlev greve Bernstorff  (1765-1766)
2 CONT # Emilie Louise Henriette comtesse von Bernstorff  (1766-1855)
2 CONT # Sophia Magdalene Charlotte comtesse von Bernstorff (1766-1769)
2 CONT # [http://www.geni.com/people/August-Johan-Hans-Hartvig-Ernst-von-Bernstorff/6000000001504723637 August Johan Hartvig Ernst rigsgreve von Bernstorff] til [http://www.gutlanken.de/ Lanken] & [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gut_Woters
2 CONC en Wotersen] (1767-1791)
2 CONT # [http://www.geni.com/people/Christian-G%C3%BCnther-von-Bernstorff/6000000011205674986Christian Günther greve von Bernstorff] til Dreilützow & Harst (1769-1835)
2 CONT # Sophia Magdalene Charlotte comtesse von Bernstorff (1770-1841)
2 CONT # [http://www.geni.com/people/Joachim-Friedrich-von-Bernstorff/6000000011206098693 Joachim Friedrich "Frederik" greve von Bernstorff] til Neuendorff (1771-1835)
2 CONT # Friedrich "Frederik" greve von Bernstorff af Dreilützow til Stintenburg & Bernstorff (1773-1838)
2 CONT # Emilie comtesse von Bernstorff (1777-1811)
2 CONT # Magnus Carl greve von Bernstorff af Bernstorff til Bernstorff (1781-1836)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Background and early career===
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Bernstorff was born in Hanover as a nephew of the statesman Count Johann Hartwig Ernst von Bernstorff whose position probably introduced him to Danish politics. After a grand European tour he 1755 joined the Danish state ser
2 CONC vice, first as a courtier and then from 1760 as a state official both in foreign political and financial matters. His career was slow but steady. During the 1760s he placed himself as an able but not outstanding official closely c
2 CONC onnected to his uncle. In the Struensee years 1770-71 he was dismissed but soon he was recalled by the new regime of Ove Høegh-Guldberg .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1773 Bernstorff was appointed Minister of Foreign Affairs and his real Danish career began. He carried through the final solution of the Gottorp question during an exchange of real estate with the Russian Tsar family together w
2 CONC ith a Russian alliance. In general he supported a pro-Russian (but independent) line trying to holdSweden in check and avoiding any conflict with England. He created an outstanding position for himself but also made many enemies w
2 CONC ithin the government. This was partly due to his alleged willfulness and partly due to political rivalry. The difficult years during the American War of Independence strengthened his wishes of an “active neutrality”. In 1780 he co
2 CONC ncluded a League of Neutrality with Russia and Sweden, a great foreign political triumph. At the same time he concluded a special agreement with England that secured Danish trade. A deep but temporary Russian dissatisfaction with 
2 CONC the treaty, however, was exploited by his Danish rivals, and in November 1780 he was dismissed by Guldberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernstorff kept in the background in 1780-84 as an interested observer of the political situation, but he still enjoyed support in Copenhagen merchant circles and did not give up his political ambitions. Very early he was connecte
2 CONC d to Crown Prince Frederick (Frederick VI) and became a part of the conspiracy against Guldberg and he supported the coup d’état of 1784 which made Frederick the Prince Regent.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Minister 1784-97===
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernstorff was for second time made Minister of Foreign Affairs in May 1784 and that opened his real golden age. Until his death he was in reality “prime minister” of Denmark, the leading man of the cabinet by whose advice the Pri
2 CONC nce Regent was normally guided. He was also temporarily the President of Danish Chancellery (Home Office)in 1788-89.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Scandinavian matters Bernstorff carried on a cautious pro-Russian policy without clashing with Sweden. The Russo-Swedish War in 1787-90 led to an abortive Danish involvement in 1788, and he managed to escape without any open br
2 CONC eaks. During the next years he gradually phased out the Russian alliance and tried to improve relations to Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernstorff's most important problem in this period was the French Revolution and the wars in its wake. He firmly maintained a neutral line and showed his special virtuosity in balancing the great powers in order to protect Danish 
2 CONC trade. He strongly kept to the International Law but avoided provocation. His diplomacy was both strong and yet not rigid, and he maintained the economic position of the Danish merchants and in spite of problems with both France a
2 CONC nd England. All the while, made his course respected. Among other things, he firmly avoided a politics of convoy while he clearly refused to take any part in the intervention in France. This was probably due to his principally neu
2 CONC tral line but also partly to his growing respect of national integrity.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Domestic politics and conclusion===
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernstorff was of course a foreign politician, but because of his leading role he clearly influenced domestic politics. Very early on he was known as a supporter of independent farmers and of the great agrarian reforms (the abolit
2 CONC ion of Adscription 1788) and other reform laws of the 1790s. A loyal supporter of Danish absolutism, he was, however, in many ways a liberal by nature and the relative freedom of the press of this period was probably due to his wi
2 CONC shes. His sympathies toward England and English political conditions – quite contrary to those of his uncle - influenced him. His cultural interests were great but he did not much influence them to any extent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernstorff is still considered one of the great Danish statesmen of the 18th century probably overshadowing his uncle. To his contemporaries his relatively early death was felt as a great misfortune – though it is impossible to de
2 CONC cide whether he would have been able to maintain his political line. Being characterised a hot-tempered and cantankerous official as a young man, he gradually emerged as a brilliant and adroit man of the world. In many ways he was
2 CONC  one of the last representatives of the German aristocratic statesmen of Danish state service before the national reaction began.
2 CONT
