1 NOTE TITEL: Greve
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Andreas Peter Bernstorff blev f°dt i 1735 og d°de i 1797.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Dansk godsejer og politiker ΓÇô oprindeligt godsejers°n fra Hannover, der fik sin uddannelse ved et tysk universitet og pσ adskillige udlandsrejser.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Bernstorff gik i 1758 ind i dansk udenrigstjeneste og blev udenrigsminister under Christian 7. i perioden 1773-80 samt 1784-97.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Bernstorff f°rte neutralitetspolitik ΓÇô og det lykkedes ham, med stor dygtighed, at holde Danmark uden for de storpolitiske opg°r. Med Bernstorff som udenrigsminister st°ttede Danmark sig til Rusland og indgik sammen med Rusland 
2 CONC i 1780 et vµbnet neutralitetsforbund med Sverige.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Det var Bernstorff der formulerede de f°rste internationale s°retsregler, bl.a.: Frit skib giver fri ladning.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Bernstorff blev i 1780 styrtet af H°egh-Guldberg, som han sammen med kronprinsen, den senere Frederik 6., igen styrtede ved et paladskup i 1784. Herefter fungerede Bernstorff i realiteten som landets egentlige leder til sin d°d.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Et nyt vµbnet neutralitetsforbund blev i 1794 indgσet med Sverige.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Under devisen sikkerhed og velstand er det er i h°j grad Bernstorff's fortjeneste, at Danmark blev holdt uden for revolutionskrigene.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Bernstorff var den drivende kraft i de landbrugsreformer der i 1788 f°rte til ophµvelsen af stavnsbσndet.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Var s°n af hannoveransk landrσd Andreas Gottlieb von Bernstorff til Gartow (1708 - 68).
2 CONT  F°dt den 28. august 1735, studerede ved universiteterne i Leipzig, Genf og G÷ttingen. Efter dette, rejste han rundt i Tyskland, Schweiz, Italien, Frankrig, England, og Holland. Under sit ophold i Italien, udnµvntes han 1754 til d
2 CONC ansk kammerjunker. 1759 kom han til K°benhavn, og fik straks sµde i det tyske cancellie, deputeret i det vestindiske-guineiske Rente- og Toldkammer og i General- Landoekonomie - og Commercecollegiet.
2 CONT  1766 deputeret i Rentekammeret og Hvid ridder. 1767 medlem af Overskattedirectionen, og med sin fader og farbror optaget i den danske grevestand.
2 CONT  1768 deputeret i det forenede Toldkammer- og Commercecollegium, hvor han skal have foranlediget at Det Afrikanske Handelsselskab ophµvedes og en ny toldrulle udfµrdiget. Da hans farbror blev afskediget, trak han sig ogsσ ud af st
2 CONC atstjenesten, hvor gerne Struensee end ville have beholdt ham, - og trak sig tilbage til sine godser i Mecklenborg.
2 CONT  Da Struensee var styrtet, blev han tilbagekaldt, og i 1772, 1ste deputeret i Rentekammeret.
2 CONT  1773 blev han udenrigsminister, prµsident i tyske cancellie m.m. Han fik udvirket at Kielerhusets linier frasagde sig enhver fordring pσ S°nderjylland, og kongen fik sσ godt som eneherred°mme over Holsteen.
2 CONT  Bσde med Sverige, England og Holland var der kurrer pσ trσden, men ved klog eftergivenhed fik han stridighederne bilagt, og Danmark var i fred med hele verden da den nordamerikanske frihedskrig br°d ud. Det drev sσ blomstrende en
2 CONC  handel pσ alle verdenshave som aldrig tidligere, og havde blandt andet en indbringende fragthandel pσ og fra de krigsf°rende lande. Dets skibe blev dog ofte foruroligede af franske og spanske, men isµr af engelske kapere, og Bers
2 CONC torff mσtte hyppigt g°re alvorlige indsigelser.
2 CONT 1778 foreslog han Sverige og Rusland at forny "den vµbnede neutralitet", og endelig den 9. juli 1780 fik han traktaten afsluttet pσ grundlaget af f°lgende sµtning: "Frit Skib gj°r frit Gods" sσ at selv fjendtlig ejendom skulle all
2 CONC evegne gσ frit i neutrale skibe, kun ikke krigsvσben og egentlige krigsforn°denheder. Neutralitetstraktaten blev forel°big Bernstorff's sidste sejr: Preussen °nskede at indtrµde i neutralitetsforbundet, men under betingelser som B
2 CONC erstorff fandt alt for gunstige; han afveg desuden i alt for mange stykker fra de °vrige medlemmeraf det Guldbergske ministerium, og den 30. november 1780 tog han atter sin afsked fra statstjenesten for at trµkke sig tilbage til M
2 CONC ecklenborg. Bernstorff var afventende, fulgte de danske begivenheder, og da kronprinsen (Frederik d. VI) havde styrtet det Guldbergske regimente, og selv overtaget regeringens t°mmer den 14. April 1784, blev Bernstorff atter indka
2 CONC ldt, og den 14. maj indtog han atter sin gamle stilling. Bernstorff n°jedes ikke med at vµre udenrigsminister, han °vede en stor indflydelse pσ alle grene af statens styrelse, nu hjalp han den unge kronprins, Colbj°rnsen og Revent
2 CONC lov med gennemf°relse af de mange landboreformer som l°ste den danske bondestand af de lµnker under hvilke den hidtil havde sukket. Det var Bernstorff selv der ved sin personlige indflydelse fremkaldte livegenskabets oph°r i hertu
2 CONC gd°mmerne, selvom loven f°rst blev gennemf°rt den 19. December 1804, syv σr efter hans d°d.
2 CONT Det er Bernstorffs fortjeneste at Danmark oplevede σr med fred og velstand, sσ regeringen fik ro til at ordne samfundsforholdene.
2 CONT I maj 1797 blev han syg, (podagra) og den 21. juni d°de Bernstorff. Mage til ligtog som det der fulgte ham til Frederikskirken pσ Christianshavn havde Danmark nµppe set f°r, og kronprinsen gik, som han selv udtrykte det: "iblandt 
2 CONC hans b°rn" Han f°lte at han have mistet en faderlig ven.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT ____________________
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Danish statesman, was born at Hanover on the 28th of August 1735. His career was determined by his uncle, Johann Hartwig Ernst Bernstorff, who early discerned the talents of his nephew and induced him to study in the German and Sw
2 CONC iss universities and travel for some years in Italy, France, England and Holland, to prepare himself for a statesmanΓÇÖs career. During these Wanderjahre he made the acquaintance of the poets Gellert and Jacobi, the learned Jean-J
2 CONC acques BarthΘlemy, the duc de Choiseul, and Gottfried Achenwall, the statistician. At his uncleΓÇÖs desire he rejected the Hanoverian for the Danish service, and in 2759 took his seat in the German chancery at Copenhagen. In 1767,
2 CONC  at the same time as his uncle, he was created a count, and in 1769 was made a privy-councillor. He is described at this period as intellectual, upright and absolutely trustworthy, but obstinate and self-opinionated to the highest
2 CONC  degree, arguing with antiquaries about coins, with equerries about horses, and with foreigners about their own countries, always certain that he was right and they wrong, whatever the discussion might be. He shared the disgrace o
2 CONC f his uncle when Struensee came into power, but re-entered the Danish service after StruenseeΓÇÖs fall at the end of 2772, working at first in the financial and economical departments, and taking an especial interest in agricultur
2 CONC e. The improvements he introduced in the tenures of his peasantry anticipated in some respects the agricultural reforms of the next generation.
2 CONT In April 1773 Bernstorff was transferred to the position for which he was especially fitted, the ministry of foreign affairs, with which he combined the presidency of the German chancery (for Schleswig-Holstein). His predecessor, 
2 CONC Adolf Siegfried Osten, had been dismissed because he was not persona grata at St Petersburg, and BernstorffΓÇÖs first official act was to conclude the negotiations which had long been pending with the grand-duke Paul as duke of Ho
2 CONC lstein-Gottorp. The result was the exchangetreaty of the 1st of June (TΓÇÖ.ΓÇÖIay 21 O.S.) 1773, confirming the previous treaty of 1767 (see BERNST0RFF, J. H. E.). This was followed by the treaty of alliance between Denmark and Ru
2 CONC ssia of the 12th of August 1773, which was partly a mutually defensive league, and partly an engagement between the two states to upset the new constitution recently established in Sweden by Gustavus III., when the right moment fo
2 CONC r doing so should arrive. For this mischievous and immoral alliance, which bound Denmark to the wheels of the Russian empressΓÇÖs chariot and sought to interfere in the internal affairs of a neighbouring state, Bernstorff was scar
2 CONC cely responsible, for the preliminaries had been definitely settled in his uncleΓÇÖs time and he merely concluded them. But there can be no doubt that he regarded this antiSwedish policy as the correct one for Denmark, especially 
2 CONC with a monarch like Gustavus III. on the Swedish throne. It is also pretty certain that the anti-Swedish alliance was RussiaΓÇÖs price for compounding the Gottorp difficulty.
2 CONT Starting from the hypothesis that Sweden was ΓÇ£DenmarkNorwayΓÇÖs most active and irreconcilable enemy,ΓÇ¥ Bernstorff logically included France, the secular ally of Sweden, among the hostile powers with whom an alliance was to be 
2 CONC avoided, and drew near to Great Britain as the natural foe of France, especially during the American War of Independence, and this too despite the irritation occasioned in Denmark-Norway by Great BritainΓÇÖs masterful interpretati
2 CONC on of the expression ΓÇ£ contraband.ΓÇ¥ BernstorffΓÇÖs sympathy with England grew stronger still when in 1779 Spain joined her enemies; and he was niuch inclined, the same winter, to join a triple alliance between Great Britain, R
2 CONC ussia and Denmark-Norway, proposed by England for the purpose of compelling the Bourbon powers to accept reasonable terms of ΓÇÿpeace. But he was overruled by the crown prince Frederick, who thought such a policy too hazardous, wh
2 CONC en Russia declined to have anything to do with it. Instead of this the Russian chancellor Nikita Panin proposed an armed league to embrace all the neutral powers, for the purpose of protecting neutral shipping in time of war. This
2 CONC  league was very similar to one proposed by Bernstorff himself in September 1778 for enforcing the principle ΓÇ£ a free ship makes the cargo free ΓÇ£;
2 CONT but as now presented by Russia, he rightly regarded it as directed exclusively against England. He acceded to it indeed (gth of July 1780) because he could not help doing so; but he had previously, by a separate treaty with Englan
2 CONC d, on the 4th of July, come to an understanding with that power as to the meaning of the expression ΓÇ£contraband of war. ΓÇ£ This independence caused great *rath at St Petersburg, where Bernstorff was accused of disloyalty, and u
2 CONC ltimately sacrificed to the resentment of the Russian government (13th of November 1780), the more readily as he already disagreed on many important points of domestic administration with the prime minister Hoegh Guldberg. He reti
2 CONC red to his Mecklenburg estates, but on the fall of Guldberg four years later, was recalled to office (April 1784). The ensuing thirteen years were perhaps the best days of the old Danish absolutism. The government, under the direc
2 CONC tion of such enlightened ministers as Bernstorff, Reventlow and others, held the mean between StruenseeΓÇÖs extravagant cosmopolitanism and GuldbergΓÇÖs stiff conservatism. In such noble projects of reform as the emancipation of t
2 CONC he serfs (see REVENTLOW) Bernstorff took a leading part, and so closely did he associate himself with everything Danish, so popular did he become in the Danish capital, that a Swedish diplomatist expressed the opinion that hencefo
2 CONC rth Bernstorff could not be removed without danger. Liberal-minded as he was, he held that ΓÇ£the will of the nation should be a law to the king,ΓÇ¥ and he boldly upheld the freedom of the press as the surest of safety-valves.
2 CONT Meanwhile foreign complications were again endangering the position of Denmark-Norway. As Bernstorff had predicted, PaninΓÇÖs neutrality project had resulted in a breach between Great Britain and Russia. Then came Gustavus IlLΓÇÖs
2 CONC  sudden war with Russia in 1788. Bernstorff was bound by treaty to assist Russia in such a contingency, but he took care that the assistance so rendered should be as trifling as possible, to avoid offending Great Britain and Pruss
2 CONC ia. Still more menacing became the political situation on the outbreak of the French Revolution. Ill-disposed as Bernstorff was towards the Jacobins. he now condemned on principle any interference in the domestic affairs of France
2 CONC , and he was persuaded that DenmarkΓÇÖs safest policy was to keep clear of every anti-French coalition. Froln this unassailable standpoint he never swerved, despite the promises and even the menaces both of the eastern and the wes
2 CONC tern powers. He was rewarded with complete success and the respect of all the diplomatists in Europe. His neutrality treaty with Sweden (17th of March 1794), for protecting their merchantmen by combined squadrons, was also extreme
2 CONC ly beneficial to the Scandinavian powers, both commercialiy and politically. Taught by the lesson of Poland, he had, in fact, long since abandoned his former policy of weakening Sweden. BernstorffΓÇÖs great faculties appeared, ind
2 CONC eed, to mature and increase with age, and his death, on the 21st of June 1797, was regarded in Denmark as a national calamity.
2 CONT Count Bernstorff was twice married, his wives being the two sisters of the writers Counts Christian and Friedrich Leopold zu Stolberg. He left seven sons and three daughters. Of his sons the best known is Christian Gunther, count 
2 CONC von Bernstorff. Another, Count Joachim, was attached to his brotherΓÇÖs fortunes so long as he remained in the Danish service, was associated with him in representing Denmark at the congress of Vienna, and in 1815 was appointed am
2 CONC bassador at that court.
