1 OCCU Sersjant
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Fredrik Christian Fredriksen Borse (1751-1802) was born on the 3rd day of Pentecost in 1751 on the farm Skotsberg in Aremark. He came to Degernes when he was 20 years old, probably as an enlisted officer within the
2 CONC  military. In the summer of 1775 he married the four-year-older Sidsel Simensdatter from Næristorp in Rakkestad. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon thereafter they leased the farm Berger in Degernes. He continued in his ancestors footprints and joined the military. He advanced to a commissioned officer, later to Fenrik.  In 1787, when he was 38 years old, he got a head i
2 CONC njury which made him unable to continue his military service. That year he was discharged from the military.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1801 census Fredrik Christian is listed as a farmer, but it was his son Nils that got the title deed to the Berger farm in 1835.
2 CONT
2 CONT The national archive contains the original application from Fredrik Christian to Major General Mansback dated July 20, 1789. The application was recommended by Pastor Hammond, Rakkestad.  In his endorsement he states that Borse is
2 CONC  an honest man – from a nationally well known military family – he descends from old Norwegian nobility – lives on a leased farm in urgent need of repair, e.t.c.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The actual application mainly reads as follows:   Dismissed Furer Fredrich Christian Borse applies sincerely for an annual pension to be    appropriated.   I, Fredrich Christian Borse, who has served for 20 years as a commissioned
2 CONC  officer of the Northern Infantry Regiment and in the Session  recently held on March 13, last,  was found to be medically unfit to further serve in His Majesty’s Military and dismissed effective March 31, 1789.  I therefore find 
2 CONC myself as a man in poor health, not raised as a farmer or used to this kind of hard work, that currently lives on leased land, on a farm in very poor condition , with my wife and 4 small children, and without any other support. I 
2 CONC therefore find it necessary to ask for help  This Condition makes me unspeakably heavy at heart but emboldens me to ask that your Highness would kindly help me to get an annual Pension because of my medical situation, and for bein
2 CONC g dismissed from my King's Service.  I also, during these hard times, lost my father, Leutnant Borse, who was the son of Field Marchal Arnholdt’s sister, and hope that I, with wife and children, will not suffer a famine. Sincerely
2 CONC  Fredrich Christian Borse  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
