2 CONT *[http://www.staatskanzler-hardenberg.de/ Biografi in Deutsch] Jura studieren in der Universität Göttingen 1766 -1770. Dort wurde er am 23. Maj 1768 in der Göttinger Freimaurerloge "Augusta zu den drei Flammen". Bekam in 1770 ein 
2 CONC Auditorenstelle. In Januar 1771 war er in die Finanzvervaltung des Kurfürstentums versetz.. 1774 heiratete er die 15-järige Juliane Christiane von Reventlow. 1781 zog er mit seiner Frau nach London.wo sich eine Affaire zwischen se
2 CONC iner frau und dem Prinzen von Wales ( dem späteren Georg iV) entwickelte. Er ließ 1790 a<uch wegen Weiter Affären seiner Frau scheiden und heiratete die seinetwegen geschiedene Sophie von Lenthe.
1 FAMC @F6000000014264059244@
1 RFN geni:6000000021494136164
1 SUBM @S6000000001503987301@
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/8b/5d/65/17/5344483c2392b084/met33qak_original.jpg?hash=31331d2c029fc4598cdb5599f5deae116c8ab19e5f573871cfff25ea021dadcd.1795939199
2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/e9/1d/0b/f1/5344483d73243f3b/karl_von_hardenberg_1__original.jpg?hash=347534ec67a801f2cd58057af47cfb6ce6c06fbb97b44f94620317c3f565c188.1795939199
2 FORM jpg
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/79/b7/3f/e3/5344483aed6edbe0/karl_august_von_hardenberg_1750_original.jpg?hash=8f6f99d1b446f34516a821498901e5d81f6bfebe5d367b3f9223e63af5b5a6f2.1795939199
2 FORM jpg
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/bc/4f/75/8e/53444838a1077cf6/hardenberg_karl_august_10044_t_original.jpg?hash=49b60da4773851c1ab61fd0076e610a4246855b202ce62587000667096bba4a6.1795939199
2 FORM jpg
1 NOTE Freiherr (baron) Von Hardenberg Prussian statesman and administrator, who preserved the integ rity of the Prussian state during the Napoleonic Wars. Domestically he was able to continue t he reforms introduced by Karl, Freiherr vo
2 CONC m Stein; in foreign affairs he exchanged Prussia' s alliance with France for an alliance with Russia in 1813, and in 1814ΓÇô15 he represented Pru ssia at the peace negotiations in Paris and Vienna. Hardenberg vainly fought for the
2 CONC  establis hment of a constitution but gained lasting fame for his liberalization of financial, economic , and agricultural policies and for his conduct of foreign affairs, which created the politic al requisites for Prussia's libe
2 CONC ration from French rule in 1813Γnswick, the oldest of seven children. He was tutored at hom e in languages, history, and geography and attended a prestigious private school in Hanover f or a year (1762ΓÇô63).
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT To prepare himself for a career in public administration, Hardenberg enrolled at the Universi ty of G÷ttingen in the fall of 1766. In 1768 he spent a year at the University of Leipzig. Wh ile there, Hardenberg attended lectures on
2 CONC  archaeology, history, literature, mathematics, th e natural sciences, and economics. He also took lessons in drawing and music, but his main fi eld was law, in which G÷ttingen provided the best instruction in GermanyΓÇöoften pavi
2 CONC ng the wa y for an appointment in the imperial civil service or in that of one of the German states.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT In 1770 Hardenberg left G÷ttingen and entered the Hanoverian Ministry of Justice. In order t o advance his career he set out in the summer of 1772ΓÇöon the advice of King George III of Eng land, who was also elector of HanoverΓÇöo
2 CONC n a year's travel throughout the whole of Germany, pri marily to widen his political horizons. In 1773 he went to England to be presented to King Ge orge III, who appointed him Hanoverian councillor.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT In 1774 Hardenberg married the 15-year-old countess Juliane von Reventlow, who bore him a so n and a daughter; they were divorced in 1788. Because his career had come to a standstill an d his wife had involved him in a scandal by 
2 CONC her liaison with the Prince of Wales, Hardenber g left the Hanoverian service and entered that of the Duke of Brunswick. There, however, he p roved to be unsuccessful as head of the department of Titelation; moreover, his personal
2 CONC  lif e became the subject of public gossip, for immediately after his divorce he had married Sophi e von Lenthe, who had been divorced from her husband on Hardenberg's account.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Prussian service.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Hardenberg therefore gladly accepted the post of Prussian provincial minister in Ansbach-Bayr euth that was offered him in 1790, a post in which he performed splendidly. He had the knac k of selecting highly capable experts and at
2 CONC tracting talented junior executives; among the fo rmer was the naturalist Alexander von Humboldt, who was in charge of the technical improvemen t of the mines. All in all Hardenberg made a model Prussian province out of the two fo
2 CONC rmer margravates.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT When, in 1798, he won the abiding trust of King Frederick William III  of Prussia, he moved t o Berlin. He was entrusted with the most important administrative and diplomatic tasks (e.g. , serving as foreign minister from 1804 to 
2 CONC 1806). Meanwhile, in Ansbach his second marriage h ad come to grief when he took his mistress into his household. She stayed with him for more t han 20 years, going with him to Berlin and later to his estate in the province of Bra
2 CONC ndenburg . He married her in 1807, six years after he had been divorced from his second wife, but shor tly before his death he also separated from her.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Up to 1806 Hardenberg advocated neutrality toward France with a view to gains in Germany. I n domestic affairs, he, like Karl vom Stein, aimed to abolish the ΓÇ£cabinetΓÇ¥ system of governm ent and to establish the departmental mi
2 CONC nisters at the expense of the cabinet counselors, b y obtaining for them direct access to the king as the king's most powerful advisers. This h e finally achieved in April 1807.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Appointment as chancellor
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT After Prussia's collapse in the war of 1806ΓÇô07 against France, Hardenberg, at Napoleon's behe st, had to surrender his ministry and to withdraw from political life. When in 1810 Prussia w as faced with insolvency and could hardl
2 CONC y maintain payments of the indemnity to Napoleon, Har denberg offered his services to restore the state's finances. He held the trust of King Frede rick William III and the sympathy of Queen Louise, and Napoleon, to whom payment o
2 CONC f the indem nity mattered most, agreed to his reinstatement. Thus, in 1810 Hardenberg became prime minist er with full powers. At the same time he supervised the ministries of the interior and financ e.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT In domestic matters Hardenberg began the second stage of the reforms inaugurated in 1807ΓÇô08 u nder Stein. After his enforced dismissal in the summer of 1807, the king had charged Hardenbe rg to draw up a report on the reorganiza
2 CONC tion of the Prussian state. He thereupon set out hi s fundamental principles for reform in the comprehensive  a monarchical regime.ΓÇ¥ He recommended these principles to the King as the on ly means of overcoming the crisis of the 
2 CONC state. The reforming legislation of 1810ΓÇô12, inspire d by Hardenberg, grew from these convictions. This legislation simplified and unified the exc ise duties, which had hitherto been levied only in the towns, and sought to impos
2 CONC e the proper ty tax on the nobility, which had previously been exempted. It also introduced freedom of tra de and a profit tax, regulated the redemption of peasant holdings from the great landed estat es, and brought civic equalit
2 CONC y for the Jews. Further legislation gave greater and more rapi d efficacy to the executive in the intermediary administrative spheres. Hardenberg even mad e a bold attempt to bring the people into closer contact with the affairs o
2 CONC f state by invitin g the cooperation of the upper middle-class citizensΓÇöin accordance with the French exampleΓÇöan d preparing a representative assembly with consultative powers. He hoped thus to win public o pinion for the gove
2 CONC rnment and its unpopular measures, so often necessary during the emergency . However, the opposition of the aristocratic landowners and their influence with the King of ten attenuated the scope of his plans. The reaction after 181
2 CONC 5 was unfavourable to the reform s and brought the agricultural reorganization to a halt, and the establishment of a represent ative assembly, which Hardenberg urged until his death, was postponed until 1847.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT If Hardenberg gave less of his energies to reform after 1812, this was because foreign polic y made ever greater claims on him. Early in 1812 Prussia had to sign a military alliance wit h France. After Napoleon's disastrous Russia
2 CONC n campaign, Hardenberg preserved the appearance o f the alliance but increased armaments and watched for the favourable moment for liberation . With great discretion, he advised the king to break away only when Prussia had an alli
2 CONC anc e with Russia. This was achieved, on the basis of the Russian proposals delivered by Stein i n February 1813, in the alliance of Kalisz. Accommodating and ready for compromise, Hardenber g, who represented Prussia in numerous 
2 CONC international negotiations between 1813 and 1822, stee red his country through the conflicting interests of the great European powers. Particularl y at the Congress of Vienna, during the crisis between the great powers over the ΓÇ
2 CONC £Saxon-Polis h question,ΓÇ¥ he was able to bring about the rapprochement between Russia and Great Britain, t he main adversaries: he yielded to pressure from Austria and Great Britain and waived the ann exation of Saxony (which Pr
2 CONC ussia had stipulated as the price of consent to Russia's designs o n Poland) in return for compensation elsewhere. In foreign affairs he associated himself, fro m 1815, with the conservative policy of the Holy Alliance. Hardenberg
2 CONC  was created prince in 1 814.
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT Assessment
2 CONT <br />
2 CONT As Hardenberg grew older, respect for his ideas increasingly declined in political circles. W hile patriots and reformers found him too accommodating and conciliatory, in the eyes of advo cates of the return to absolutist rule he 
2 CONC was too liberal. By 1822 his great diplomatic achie vements and notable domestic reforms of 1810ΓÇô13 had been largely forgotten. Later in the 19t h century the great German historian Leopold von Ranke <article?idxref=71787> was t
2 CONC o emphasiz e Hardenberg's achievements as a statesman, pointing out that he had preserved the Prussian s tate when it was on the verge of destruction at Napoleon's hands. Since then Hardenberg has b een primarily remembered in tha
2 CONC t role. While the social progress achieved by his reform legis lation has always been acknowledged, it has been truly appreciated only in the 20th century.
