1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Apollonius of Maltitz was the youngest son of the imperial Russian envoy (in Portugal, Württemberg and Baden), Peter Friedrich (Pjotr Fyodorovich) of Maltitz (1753–1826) and the Apollo Agnes Martha of Maltitz, née 
2 CONC from Lieven from the Bersen House (1765–1826), who had inherited the Dursuppen estate.[
2 CONT He was also in the Russian diplomatic service and married Mary Elizabeth Frances Lee (1799–1852), the youngest daughter of the Secretary of State in the US State Department, William Lee,[[2] and was most recently Russian ambassado
2 CONC r to The Hague. From there because of his strong Catholic tendency (he had converted into Berlin in 1831),[3] he retired to Boppard in 1854, where he was on the 25th century. died in April 1857. He has made himself known in German
2 CONC y through a supplement to Schiller's Demetrius - Fragments (1817).
2 CONT
2 CONT The younger son Apollonius was born on a journey of his parents in Gera and was educated by tutors, but his education was mainly appropriated in self-study. With his parents and siblings, he came to London early, then to Hamburg a
2 CONC nd in 1806 to the Kingdom of Westphalia in Kassel. The following years until 1812 the family spent mostly in Dresden and moved to Stuttgart during the .
2 CONT
2 CONT Since the sons of Peter Friedrich von Maltitz had no [descendants, Apollonius of Maltitz had this Baltic-German free-gloric family branch.
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollonius_von_Maltitz
