1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Harald Andersson Appelbom=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Anders Haraldsson Appelbom and Carin Andersdotter, (Hök af Partille) 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Appelbom_nr_325 Harald Appelbom (son of Anders Haraldsson, ennobled Appelbom, by Adelsvapen Genealogies]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''TABLE 2'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Harald Appelbom (son of Anders Haraldsson, ennobled Appelbom,''' tab 1 ). Born in 1612 in Söderby. Burgrave in Gothenburg 1653. Court council 19-12-1663. '''Married in 1645 in Amsterdam to Susanne Roseau.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Harald Appelbom,''' born 1646, died young.
2 CONT * '''Carl Appelbom,''' born 1647. Court eunuch. Resident with the States General in 1674. Married in Holland and died childless.
2 CONT * '''A daughter.''' Married to the city engineer of Riga and the captain of the fortification Fredrik Statius von Dahlen .
2 CONT * '''Otto Johan Appelbom.''' Captain in Dutch service 1672. Died unmarried before his father.
2 CONT * '''A daughter.''' Married in Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/18748#/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/18751 Harald Appelboom,Burgomaster, Diplomat, by The Swedish Biographical Dictionary]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Harald Appelboom, b. 1612 at Söderby in Fellingsbro parish, d. 8 March 1674 in The Hague. Parents: county sheriff Anders Haraldsson, ennobled Appelbom, and Karin Andersdotter Hök till Partilla. Enrolled in 1631 at the Universit
2 CONC y of Franeker in the Netherlands. Correspondent of the Swedish government in Amsterdam 1 Oct. 1642 (renewed plenipotentiary 12 Dec. 1652); envoy to England May–June 1652; resident in the United Netherlands from Feb. 1653; burgrave
2 CONC  of Gothenburg 30 July 1665. but never exercised this office, which he did not formally resign until 1664; court councilor 19 Dec. 1663; envoy 15 Feb. 1665.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Married 1645 to Susanna Roseau from Holland.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT As Swedish agent in Amsterdam, A.'s main task was to guard Sweden's trade interests in Holland. He began his purely political activities in 1650, from which time he served as deputy to the Swedish resident in The Hague. Initially,
2 CONC  his main task was to try to prevent the ratification of the so-called Redemption Treaty between Denmark and Holland. He also received orders from his government to mediate peace between the Netherlands and Portugal. An important 
2 CONC diplomatic mission fell to A. in 1652, when, after the death of Peter Spiering, he was ordered to continue his negotiations in London, the first diplomatic connection between Sweden and the English republic ruled by Cromwell. He h
2 CONC as left an apt description of personalities and conditions there. From London, A. returned to The Hague in June 1652, where he later remained as Sweden's diplomatic representative until his death. In 1653, he made an offer for Swe
2 CONC den's mediation between England and the Netherlands. Some of A.'s dispatches from the summer of 1657, in which he spoke of the advisability of winning over Dutch statesmen with bribes, were intercepted by the Danes and brought to 
2 CONC the attention of the Dutch, who were at that time working to provoke a break between Sweden and the Netherlands. The letters aroused great resentment in Holland, and proposals were made to break off relations with A. However, Char
2 CONC les X Gustav defended his minister, and the Dutch were soon persuaded to let the matter fall through. During the disputes between Sweden and the Netherlands in 1658–60, A. received repeated requests from the king to return home, b
2 CONC ut nevertheless remained in The Hague and continued to guard Sweden's interests with great energy. Together with Kr. Delphicus von Dohna, he signed the treaty in The Hague on 18 July 1667 on the annulment of the so-called elucidat
2 CONC ions to the Elbing Treaty. In 1668 he was commissioned to mediate in the war between France and Spain and, together with Dohna, conducted the negotiations that led to Sweden's accession to the Triple Alliance. On 7 May 1669, A. si
2 CONC gned the treaty with England and the Netherlands guaranteeing the peace of Aachen. In 1672 he offered his mediation in the war between France and Holland, which was also accepted. — In the diplomatic world of his time, A. occupied
2 CONC  a notable position. His long stay in Holland gave him a particularly thorough knowledge of the conditions of that country, and through his extensive personal connections within various political camps there he achieved considerab
2 CONC le influence. He could justly boast of being the best informed of all the diplomats in The Hague. Partly through the pecuniary sacrifices he made to obtain secret papers from the Office of the States General, he found himself in a
2 CONC  financial predicament that was never fully remedied by the fees and support he received from home.
2 CONT
2 CONT A.'s extensive diplomatic correspondence is kept in the National Archives; Uppsala University Library owns his letters to Krister Bonde in 1655 and 1656 (Nordinska saml. vol. 187 and 188) as well as a summary account of his diplom
2 CONC atic activities during the years 1649–63, kept among the papers of K. Rådet E. Ehrenstéen. His translations of Bidpais' (Pilpais') fables (1663) and J. Barclay's »Argenis» (c. 1670), which are found in manuscript in many places in
2 CONC  public and private collections, testify to literary interests, which he shared with his brother, the governor and war chamber councilor Anders Appelbom (b. 1614, d. 1686), known as the translator of J. Lütkemans »Harpa aff tijo s
2 CONC tränger, thet är: En grundelig förlågning öfwer tijo konung Davids psalmer» (1678).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Swedish Biographical Lexicon (SBL). Gustaf Elgenstierna , The introduced genealogical tables of the Swedish nobility. 1925-36.
